  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Bob can verify and locally record a signed envelope, but this specimen does not define what an unauthorized Bob does next in ring terms: break, skip, refuse, or reconfigure. It mainly provides a clear envelope/dispatch layer rather than a transport authorization model. Deterministic CBOR and mandatory signatures help provenance, while unknown-pCID hard reject and mandatory sig_pCID make mixed-version evolution brittle.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit pCID dispatch cleanly separates envelope handling from payload semantics.",
      "Deterministic CBOR plus mandatory signature/proof slots support stable local verification.",
      "Avoids reliance on a central registry or globally complete state."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No built-in per-hop authorization, refusal receipt, or ring-membership behavior.",
      "Scenario outcome depends on an unspecified wrapper or payload protocol.",
      "Hop-local evidence placement is underspecified across wrappers, payloads, and signature-related machinery."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Unknown-pCID hard reject can turn authorization or signature upgrades into dropped traffic.",
      "Without a standard signed refusal artifact, later auditors may not know why Carol never received the message.",
      "Different implementations may choose incompatible conventions for hop-local evidence."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should Bob emit a standardized signed refusal record when he is not authorized to forward?",
      "Should per-hop authorization and membership reconfiguration live in a wrapper protocol or the payload protocol?",
      "Can hard-reject nodes preserve opaque evidence for audit or migration without treating it as a valid envelope?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
